"""Editorial source. Each topic has twelve explanations and four review chapters.

Fields: title, slide takeaway, guide/notes explanation, editable diagram labels.
"""
VOLUMES=[
dict(topic='artwork-to-applications',title='From artwork to applications',subtitle='One image. Thirty-two colors. Fifteen native destinations.',refs=['README.md','docs/ARCHITECTURE.md','docs/OUTPUT-REFERENCE.md','docs/OUTPUT-NAMING.md'],chapters=[
('The image is the starting point','Keep the original bytes and a receipt.','The engine preserves the selected image under the run source directory and records its SHA-256. Decoding creates working pixels without replacing the input. That separation lets a reviewer identify the artwork even after application tokens have changed. Start a review by comparing the preserved source with the image you intended to use. A familiar display name alone cannot establish that identity.','Original image|Working pixels|Source receipt'),
('Five layers tell different stories','Artwork, candidates, canonical colors, roles, and tokens have separate jobs.','Candidates describe observed image colors. The canonical palette selects exactly 32 recorded identities. Semantics assign those identities to purposes such as background or warning. Adaptation produces application values, sometimes changing lightness or chroma. Keep these records separate when explaining a result: a target swatch is not the original canonical palette, and a role name does not prove that a conventional hue existed in the image.','Pixels|Canonical 32|Semantic roles|Target tokens'),
('Fifteen destinations, different contracts','A terminal palette and a browser stylesheet solve different problems.','The default target set spans terminals, document editors, native editor formats, PowerPoint, browser interfaces, SVG, charts, and Kmindz. Every selected target emits its native payload and supporting palette, tokens, swatch, and validation. File extensions describe delivery formats, not interchangeability. A CSS theme cannot serve as a native terminal schema, and an Office theme cannot express every editor state.','Terminals|Documents|Visuals|Browser UI'),
('A run is a reviewable bundle','Read run.json before hunting for files.','The run summary indexes authoritative target manifests. Those inventories supply actual filenames, logical roles, and hashes. Root-level review.html makes the run approachable, while palette.toml and semantics.json retain inspectable data. Copy a complete target directory for a portable specimen so its relative CSS, script, and font dependencies remain intact. Treat filenames constructed from old examples as hints rather than current authority.','run.json|Target manifest|Native payload|Review artifacts'),
('Generation and import begin differently','Generate extracts. Import preserves supplied canonical values.','Generation decodes an image and selects a new canonical set. Import reads a 32-color palette and adapts it without re-extracting the artwork. Import can still produce different semantic assignments under a newer engine. Choose import when comparing native destinations for an existing palette. Choose generation when the image or selection procedure is intentionally changing, and review any ID-based overrides afterward.','Generate image|Select 32|Import palette|Adapt targets'),
('Darkness can carry image identity','The darkest pixel is not always the default surface.','The semantic background policy prefers suitable dark image colors near its configured lightness. This can preserve a chromatic identity that pure black would hide. An explicit darkest policy and canonical-ID overrides are available. The canonical palette stays unchanged whichever surface policy is selected. Inspect the actual background reference and target surface derivations instead of assuming color_01 must be the application background.','Dark candidates|Identity surface|Foreground|Nested surfaces'),
('Office exposes twelve theme slots','Documentation can use more recorded colors than the sample visibly uses.','The exporter preserves the supplied editable PowerPoint structure and binds twelve native Office color slots. The accompanying target palette and token records contain additional usable values, while the run retains all 32 canonical colors. These volumes keep the Office scheme and apply recorded colors to new editable objects. A visible swatch is labeled as canonical or target-derived so palette breadth does not erase provenance.','12 Office slots|Target tokens|32 canonical colors'),
('Visual outputs are usable material','Reuse real specimens and label their purpose.','The SVG target supplies six editable compositions. Data visualization provides bars, lines, a heatmap, a style, and scales. Syntax highlighting packages grammar-backed examples. These outputs are rich documentation assets, but their sample values and fictional scenes remain illustrative. A picture of a working specimen proves that rendering occurred in that environment, not that another native application has accepted the theme.','SVG geometry|Chart fixtures|Syntax specimens|Native packages'),
('Identity survives collection','apt, dnf, and rpm identify artwork generations.','The generation prefix belongs to theme identity and is independent of the engine package version. The selected BlackGold run uses rpm, while Crystal, Factor, and Swift use apt. Current manifests establish the exact names used here. Preserve physical directories and source receipts when collecting artifacts; normalizing every prefix to apt would silently change the meaning of the collection.','Generation|Slug|Source hash|Physical run'),
('Architecture makes custody explicit','CLI, pipeline, adaptation, and dispatch have distinct owners.','The command layer handles arguments and presentation. Pipeline orchestration preserves inputs and assembles the run. Target adaptation assigns native values, and exporter dispatch writes specialized formats. Shared transactions handle staged commits and recovery. Atlas snapshots existing runs, while finishing processes explicit PNG selections. This arrangement explains where to investigate a problem without treating every utility as a theme generator.','CLI|Pipeline|Adaptation|Exporters'),
('A review has several gates','Package validity and native suitability answer different questions.','Static checks inspect schemas, references, declared contrast pairs, and file inventories. Browser rendering checks actual web behavior. Native import and rendering check a particular application. Manual edit/save/reopen, installation, and publication need additional evidence. Record the environment and date for each gate. A historical passing record remains useful evidence, but it cannot stand in for a new acceptance run.','Static|Browser|Native render|Manual round trip'),
('Follow one color through the bundle','Read its source ID before interpreting its application use.','The worked lineage figure traces the recorded primary role to its canonical color, then to the PowerPoint primary token and Office accent1. Compare exact HEX values and the derived flag. A difference can be an intentional adaptation rather than corruption. Continue into the copied token and palette artifacts to see requested coordinates, purpose, and gamut information. The same reading method applies to other target roles.','Canonical ID|Primary role|PowerPoint token|Office accent1'),
('Decision: preserve breadth','Show all 32 while using readable combinations.','A complete palette spread documents the source set. Working diagrams use colors for structure and labels for meaning. Dark swatches receive readable labels outside the fill. Choosing a subset for a particular slide is intentional; forcing all colors into every figure would obscure the explanation. Keep the palette spread as the exact reference and use token records for application surfaces.','Complete palette|Selected accents|Readable labels'),
('Failure: plausible files can mislead','A valid container can still be the wrong artifact.','A copied filename, a mismatched generation prefix, or an unverified source image can produce an apparently credible document. Resolve artifacts through inventories and verify hashes before authoring. If a source changes during the work, stop using the old receipt and capture an intentional revision. Do not repair the baseline by replacing it with the changed files.','Identity mismatch|Hash mismatch|Fresh revision'),
('Evidence: keep dates attached','This collection records documentation evidence separately.','The new deck and guide describe inspected local source and selected existing outputs. Older project acceptance remains dated. Native rendering of these documentation decks establishes their rendering in the recorded PowerPoint environment. It does not install exported themes or renew acceptance for every application mentioned in the guide. Read the collection validation directory for actual checks and outstanding limits.','Source snapshot|Delivery checks|Historical records'),
('Recovery: keep a prior revision','A fresh destination preserves a usable comparison.','A successful pipeline overwrite replaces the prior run, even though failed commits can roll back. Preserve dated outputs when history matters. This documentation collection uses a separate directory and hashes the input runs before and after authoring. Future revisions should create a new sibling collection, update references deliberately, and retain the earlier package as evidence.','Prior run|Fresh destination|Compare|Retain history')]),
dict(topic='canonical-32',title='Finding the canonical 32',subtitle='Observed color becomes a stable, inspectable palette.',refs=['apt_theme/extraction.py','apt_theme/colors.py','profiles.toml','docs/OUTPUT-REFERENCE.md'],chapters=[
('Decode without replacing the source','Normalize working pixels and retain original bytes.','ImageMagick reads the first frame, applies orientation and an sRGB profile, enables alpha, and exports 8-bit RGBA working pixels. The original dimensions and input hash remain recorded. A 16-bit original therefore stays preserved even though the extraction working representation is 8-bit. Do not describe the canonical values as unmodified samples of the original high-bit-depth encoding.','Original bytes|Orientation and ICC|sRGB RGBA'),
('Color management is part of input validity','Unprofiled CMYK is rejected.','The extractor inspects colorspace and embedded profiles before conversion. CMYK input without an embedded ICC profile cannot provide a defensible conversion and raises an error. Profiled inputs use the documented sRGB working conversion. Keep the normalization receipt beside the palette so two runs using different source encodings can be compared without assuming that their channel numbers meant the same thing.','Inspect input|Require CMYK ICC|Convert to sRGB'),
('Transparency changes population','Invisible pixels contribute no colors.','Fully transparent pixels are removed before sampling. Remaining RGB values are deduplicated, and their population weights sum alpha divided by 255. A partially transparent color therefore contributes less weight than an equally frequent opaque color. This policy protects selection from hidden RGB data, but sampled populations still describe the bounded normalized input rather than every possible composited background.','Exclude alpha zero|Sample visible pixels|Weight opacity'),
('Bounded sampling makes work reproducible','Use the configured limit and seed.','The default sample limit is 65,536 visible pixels with seed 32. Larger inputs use a reproducible random subset without replacement, sorted back into pixel order. The limit must allow at least 32 samples. These choices bound clustering cost. Rare details can be absent from the sample, so compare the artwork visually and retain candidate diagnostics instead of treating deterministic output as exhaustive coverage.','Visible pixels|Seeded subset|Unique RGB'),
('Perceptual coordinates guide clustering','Distances use OKLab coordinates.','Encoded sRGB channels are linearized before the OKLab transformation. Clustering and spacing use perceptual coordinates rather than raw RGB channel distance. OKLCH supplies lightness, chroma, and hue descriptions for subsequent policies. Hue near neutrality is poorly informative, so a small numerical hue difference should not be presented as a strong visual distinction between nearly gray colors.','sRGB|Linear RGB|OKLab|OKLCH'),
('Candidates stay observed','Cluster centers guide selection but are not exported colors.','Population-weighted KMeans forms up to the configured candidate count, normally 192. Each cluster contributes its nearest observed member, carrying the cluster population weight. Representatives are sorted by lightness and HEX. This avoids exporting a centroid color that never appeared in the normalized sample. The representative is observed in working sRGB pixels, not necessarily a byte sequence in the original encoded image.','Weighted KMeans|Nearest observed member|Candidate population'),
('Near-black noise has a bounded policy','Keep one darkest anchor when enough alternatives remain.','Candidates whose three 8-bit sRGB channels are all at most 12 form the near-black noise floor. If collapsing them still leaves at least 32 candidates, the pool retains the observed darkest anchor and combines their weights. Sparse inputs preserve their candidates. This is a selection policy, not recoloring: retained RGB values are unchanged. Read selection diagnostics to see whether coalescing occurred.','Near-black pool|Enough alternatives?|Observed anchor'),
('Thirty-two selections combine anchors and diversity','Darkest and lightest anchors remain visible in the method.','Selection begins with the lowest and highest OKLab lightness candidates. Thirty weighted clusters suggest additional representatives, and a 0.03 spacing threshold avoids near-duplicate additions. The algorithm fills remaining slots using population-aware distance from selected colors. Explain these rules together: neither pure popularity nor farthest-point diversity alone describes the implemented method.','Dark/light anchors|Cluster representatives|Diversity fill'),
('Ordering creates neutral identities','color_01 through color_32 follow selected lightness order.','Selected candidates are ordered by lightness, with HEX as a tie-breaker, then assigned canonical IDs. An ID is stable within that recorded palette and is not a universal role across themes. Re-running extraction after changing the image or algorithm may change the ID ordering. Review overrides that name IDs before carrying them into a newly generated run.','Selected colors|Lightness order|Canonical IDs'),
('Diagnostics quantify limited questions','Coverage and spacing do not score beauty.','Weighted mean OKLab distance describes how close the normalized visible sample lies to the selected palette. Maximum sample distance highlights the least well represented sampled color. Minimum palette spacing identifies the closest selected pair. These metrics aid comparison, but they cannot establish aesthetic quality, native text readability, or whether a meaningful tiny feature was absent from the sample.','Mean distance|Maximum distance|Minimum spacing'),
('Import is a preservation path','Exactly 32 entries and dark variants are required.','The loader accepts palette colors represented as HEX, RGB, or OKLCH and understands UTF-8 BOM input. HEX takes precedence when present. Imported IDs and canonical RGB values are preserved, while legacy family and role information are retained as context. Invalid references, duplicate IDs, insufficient entries, or a light variant fail validation. Import does not prove that a palette came from the image displayed beside it.','Read palette|Validate 32|Preserve IDs|Adapt'),
('Read the Crystal fixture as recorded evidence','Inspect candidates and selection beside the image.','Use the copied Crystal palette, candidates, selection diagnostics, and source receipt to inspect the actual retained run. The palette spread and lightness figure use its exact recorded values. This collection does not rerun Crystal extraction or invent a TIFF comparison. The historical Zig TIFF and Black-Gold regression fixtures serve different purposes and must retain their own source identities.','Crystal artwork|Recorded candidates|Selected 32|Diagnostics'),
('Decision: observed colors constrain design','Missing hues are a finding, not an invitation to invent.','Extraction selects from observed working colors. A narrow image may not supply every conventional status hue or enough distinct colors. Preserve that constraint in the guide and discuss semantic fallbacks later. Do not add a new hue family to make an extraction diagram look more balanced or to conceal a sparse input failure.','Observed set|Explicit limits|Semantic findings'),
('Failure: fewer than 32 visible colors','Stop with a clear input error.','The extractor rejects an empty visible image or fewer than 32 distinct sampled RGB colors. Raising a target token budget cannot solve a canonical input failure because target adaptation happens later. Choose a suitable image or deliberately import a valid existing palette. Do not duplicate colors to make a file appear to contain 32 useful candidates.','Input validation|Clear failure|Choose another source'),
('Evidence: repeatability has conditions','Record source, settings, and engine revision.','A fixed seed contributes to reproducibility, but identical input bytes, normalization, settings, dependencies, and algorithm are also relevant. The source hash is rechecked after extraction. For comparisons, retain both runs and their diagnostics. Native application rendering is an independent question and cannot validate the clustering algorithm or its aesthetic choices.','Source bytes|Settings|Engine revision|Diagnostics'),
('Recovery: revisit overrides after regeneration','A canonical ID can move when selection changes.','Keep the previous palette and its override configuration together. Generate a new revision into a separate destination, compare the selected colors and ID assignments, and review each role override against the new palette. If the goal is to preserve the original 32 while exploring another target, import that palette instead of regenerating from the image.','Previous palette|New generation|Review IDs|Retain both')]),
dict(topic='semantic-interfaces',title='Turning colors into interfaces',subtitle='Separate source identity, semantic intent, and native values.',refs=['apt_theme/semantics.py','apt_theme/targets.py','apt_theme/target_palette.py','docs/POWERPOINT.md'],chapters=[
('Canonical IDs carry identity','Roles carry purpose.','The semantic record maps names such as background, primary, and warning to canonical IDs. It does not rewrite the palette. Several roles can legitimately reference one source color. In the Factor example, inspect the role table and compare repeated references rather than interpreting a role count as a count of distinct hues. This separation makes an override auditable.','Canonical ID|Semantic role|Target value'),
('Identity surfaces use a policy','Default lightness is 0.20 within a dark search range.','Background selection searches canonical colors in the 0.12 to 0.32 lightness range and prefers a chromatic pool when suitable entries exist. Distance from the desired lightness, chroma penalty, and population affect ranking. The darkest policy is explicit. A background override takes precedence and changes the context used for foreground and secondary-text assignment.','Dark range|Chromatic preference|Weighted choice'),
('Foreground follows contrast','Brightness alone does not name every text role.','Foreground selection considers contrast against the chosen background. Muted text aims near a readable contrast region rather than arbitrary opacity. The adapter can then adjust text against multiple surfaces. Inspect actual emitted values and declared checks before using a color in a figure. A semantic source assignment does not guarantee that its unadapted RGB value is readable everywhere.','Background|Foreground choice|Surface checks'),
('Accents balance prominence and distance','Primary and secondary serve different visual jobs.','Primary selection favors chromatic colors with population and chroma influence. Secondary favors perceptual distance from that prominent source. These are deterministic heuristics, not an endorsement of a brand hierarchy. Preserve the recorded roles when describing an existing run. If an operator deliberately overrides them, capture the configuration and compare the resulting target records in a new run.','Chromatic candidates|Primary prominence|Secondary distance'),
('Status names do not guarantee conventional hues','Keep the warning when a hue is unavailable.','Status and ANSI role selection score available chromatic candidates against preferred hues and lightness. A hue distance above the implemented threshold produces an unavailable_conventional_hue finding. The selected value remains image-derived. Pair color with explicit text and symbols in actual interfaces. Renaming an orange source green would conceal a limitation rather than create a conventional success color.','Preferred hue|Available source|Finding|Labeled status'),
('Budgets bound named tokens','Unique colors can be fewer than token names.','Each target has a default budget and required roles. Aliases mean several tokens can share one HEX value; omitted canonical IDs identify sources unused by that target. The pipeline rejects a budget below mandatory needs instead of dropping required states silently. Use actual named_count and unique_count from a run when presenting quantities rather than quoting the planning budget as output size.','Budget|Mandatory tokens|Aliases|Omissions'),
('Adaptation preserves lineage','Derived does not mean canonical mutation.','Target generation can adjust lightness and chroma, seek readable contrast, and fit values into sRGB. Each token records its canonical source, purpose, requested coordinates, gamut flag, and whether its HEX differs from the source. The root palette stays unchanged. A provenance diagram should show both values when they differ, including the exact source ID so the transformation can be inspected.','Canonical RGB|Lightness/chroma|Gamut fit|Token origin'),
('Surface checks are contextual','Nested panels can become the hardest text background.','For non-terminal targets, adaptation considers background, panel, and elevated surfaces while seeking text readability. Additional checks inspect syntax and foreground values on nested surfaces. Selection text has its own background pair. These are declared opaque-color checks. Host opacity, overlay composition, or custom surfaces can change real contrast and require additional review.','Base surface|Panel|Elevated|Selection'),
('Terminal output protects twenty roles','ANSI slots and application colors have a native shape.','Windows Terminal and Alacritty use eight ANSI colors, their bright counterparts, and four application roles. The adaptation checks text and selection pairs, while schemas differ by application. An ANSI name may map to an image-derived approximation when the hue is unavailable. Terminal output is a useful worked reduction example because its mandatory contract is explicit and compact.','16 ANSI slots|Background|Foreground|Cursor and selection'),
('Office bindings stay native','Twelve slots are a reusable scheme.','PowerPoint exports an Office color scheme and preserves the supplied sample slides, master/layout relationships, charts, and embedded workbooks. Theme slots can alias by design. Documentation objects may explicitly use additional recorded colors while preserving the scheme. The illustrative chart data in the original template is not project performance evidence and is not reused as such in these volumes.','Office scheme|Native layouts|Editable objects|Recorded colors'),
('Target palettes are separate artifacts','A target swatch describes emitted values.','The target palette TOML records actual token order, alias groups, omitted canonical sources, identity, and transformation details. Its swatch follows those target values. The canonical spread remains the source reference. When handing off a target, include its inventory and supporting artifacts so downstream readers can inspect why a state color differs from the original source.','Canonical palette|Target palette|Swatch|Validation'),
('Trace Factor primary end to end','Compare the semantic reference, token, and native binding.','Read Factor semantics.json to find the primary source. Locate that ID in palette.toml, then inspect the PowerPoint token origin and accent1 in the Office XML. The lineage figure uses those actual values and reports whether adaptation changed the HEX. Repeat with a status token to see why a role can carry a hue-availability finding even when its declared contrast checks pass.','Factor ID|Primary role|Token origin|accent1'),
('Decision: use labels with narrow palettes','A color name cannot carry the entire meaning.','A narrow image family may cause repeated status values or close categorical series. Use words, markers, patterns, and shape differences in actual outputs. Do not fabricate a new hue family to make the diagram look conventional. Palette identity and clear interface meaning can coexist when color is one cue among several.','Color|Text|Marker|Pattern'),
('Failure: a budget can be too small','Retain mandatory native behavior or reject the request.','Budgets are not a request to pad colors or discard required roles. The adapter checks mandatory token counts and fails when a target cannot retain them. Review the target contract before changing a configuration. A larger budget does not promise more unique colors because legitimate aliases remain possible.','Requested budget|Required roles|Pass or reject'),
('Evidence: lineage differs from consumption','A recorded token does not prove native property usage.','Token records explain generation. Native files show serialization. Actual selector/property consumption requires inspecting the exporter and application behavior. Atlas can display descendants but does not invent that usage evidence. Keep each layer visible when reviewing an adaptation so an impressive origin graph cannot be mistaken for a native acceptance result.','Origin record|Serialized file|Application behavior'),
('Recovery: change a mapping in a new run','Preserve the comparison and its configuration.','Copy the relevant override into a reviewed configuration and generate or import into a fresh destination. Compare semantic references, target token values, findings, and native output. Keep the original run for rollback. These documentation volumes explain that workflow and do not change any mappings in the selected theme runs.','Override config|Fresh run|Compare artifacts|Retain original')]),
dict(topic='themes-on-web',title='Themes on the web',subtitle='From scoped CSS to an intentional delivery boundary.',refs=['docs/WEBSITE-TARGET.md','docs/HOSTING.md','apt_theme/website.py','docs/COMMAND-CONTRACT.md'],chapters=[
('The website target is a browser contract','Scoped CSS and reference DOM travel together.','The website target produces a reusable dark theme with opt-in component classes. It is not a production site or a framework adapter. Read the emitted integration guide and actual specimens together: CSS variables describe values, while markup shows the component relationships expected by selectors. Preserve that context when incorporating the output into another application.','.apt-site|--apt-* variables|.apt-* components'),
('Scope contains visual influence','Apply the wrapper before adding components.','Place .apt-site on a body or containing element, then opt into components such as navigation, buttons, cards, forms, and tables. The scoped namespace reduces unintended spillover. Host CSS can still override inherited properties, reset rules, or component behavior. Include an unrelated control region during integration review to reveal leakage and verify that the surrounding application remains readable.','Themed wrapper|Component subtree|Unrelated control'),
('States are part of the palette','Default, hover, pressed, and focus need readable behavior.','Website adaptation extends shared tokens for actual component and interaction states. Read the exported tokens and validation rather than deriving arbitrary transparent overlays. Native control popups and host-specific styling may differ from the reference browser. Keyboard focus must remain visible, and a color-only change should not be the sole explanation of state.','Default|Hover|Pressed|Focus'),
('The landing specimen shows composition','Treat it as a reference, not a deployed product.','The landing page combines navigation, a hero, content blocks, surfaces, a footer, and disclosure elements. It demonstrates how the generated CSS fits a real page. The copy and scene remain sample material. Reuse the structure deliberately, replace illustrative text with project facts, and check long headings, small screens, and actual content lengths in the host.','Navigation|Hero|Content|Footer'),
('The component specimen shows behavior','Forms and dialogs add interaction questions.','Buttons, inputs, messages, tables, code, selection, and a native dialog exercise more than a palette swatch can. Opening and closing a dialog, Escape handling, visible focus, and form feedback need browser checks. Those checks establish the specimen behavior in the measured environment. They do not establish screen-reader acceptance or every operating-system popup state.','Controls|Dialog|Messages|Keyboard review'),
('Offline delivery includes dependencies','Copy the complete target tree.','The website specimens use local CSS and scripts without external runtime services. Other browser targets may include nested vendor resources. Copying only an HTML page can break its relative references. Keep the target inventory, supporting metadata, and dependency directories together, then check the copied version with network requests blocked or observed.','HTML|CSS|Local scripts|Supporting records'),
('Manifest discovery beats filename guessing','Find the actual landing and stylesheet entries.','Current filenames include generation, theme, and target identity. Resolve the website manifest through run.json, then use logical entries to locate the page and CSS. Documentation examples using generic theme.css or landing.html may describe logical roles or historical runs. A portable collection should link to the actual copied filenames and verify their hashes before presenting them.','run.json|Website manifest|Actual filename|Hash'),
('Hosting belongs to another owner','The engine generates files; the server exposes selected paths.','The hosting guide describes a read-only output mount and allowlisted serving controlled under A:/webserver. Those are recorded operational arrangements, not actions performed by theme generation. A generated review file can become public if it is edited inside an already served path. Keep documentation builds and experimental runs outside public directories unless a separate publication action is authorized.','Engine output|Read-only mount|Allowlisted route|Public boundary'),
('A hostname is not a publication receipt','Check route, bytes, and denied paths when publishing.','Recorded hosting evidence distinguishes origin routing, public tunnel delivery, authored HTML transformations, downloadable bytes, and intentional 404 responses. This volume explains the historical contract without rechecking live service state. Before a future release, inspect current server configuration and verify only the intended paths. A local browser preview alone cannot establish the public URL or its access restrictions.','Origin route|Public response|Byte checks|Denied paths'),
('A safe preview has its own destination','Use a fresh local directory and loopback when needed.','Theme generation and this documentation build preserve existing hosted copies. Review standalone HTML directly when supported. For ES modules, use a loopback server confined to the copied collection. Do not bind a development server broadly or modify server routes merely to read a specimen. Separate preview evidence from a future publication decision.','Fresh directory|Local preview|Review|Separate release'),
('Dark themes still need integration review','Host changes can invalidate a declared color pair.','Declared checks assume the generated values and known backgrounds. Host opacity, gradients, overlays, browser defaults, and CSS overrides can change effective contrast. Inspect computed values and actual keyboard behavior at representative screen widths. Keep status labels and meaningful text visible when conventional hues are absent. Passing generation validation is a starting point for that review.','Generated values|Host CSS|Computed result|Interaction'),
('Read Caddy as an output specimen','Follow its actual files and supporting token records.','Open the copied Caddy website index and compare the component page with its swatch and token JSON. Trace one foreground or focus token to its source ID. The new collection links the specimen for exploration but does not modify its CSS or renew the historical browser acceptance. Follow the guide procedure to test a real host independently.','Caddy manifest|Component page|Token record|Source ID'),
('Decision: scope before integration','Keep selectors tied to the intended subtree.','A scoped contract provides a controlled starting point for a host application. Preserve .apt-site and the generated component namespace instead of promoting every selector into a global reset. Review nested containers and unrelated controls. Framework adaptation is a separate implementation task with its own native contract and acceptance limits.','Wrapper|Opt-in components|Isolation check'),
('Failure: incomplete copies break offline use','Missing CSS can look like a broken theme.','When a specimen loses styling or interaction, compare the copied files with the manifest before changing tokens. Check relative paths and nested dependencies. Recopy verified original bytes into a fresh review directory rather than patching the source run. A missing file problem and an unsuitable color mapping require different remedies.','Missing dependency|Manifest comparison|Verified copy'),
('Evidence: historical serving stays dated','This package does not change public delivery.','Hosting claims in this volume are attributed to project records and their dates. The delivery validation covers the local collection and its links. No server reload, route change, public asset copy, or live endpoint verification is part of the build. Future publication must establish current route behavior and intended access boundaries independently.','Dated record|Local evidence|Future verification'),
('Recovery: restore reviewed configuration selectively','Do not overwrite later server changes from an old backup.','The hosting guide identifies recovery copies and historical configuration. A future rollback must compare those records with the current server state before restoring anything. Keep unrelated services and persistent volumes intact. Documentation authoring needs no such operation and leaves the server workspace unchanged.','Recorded backup|Current comparison|Selective restore')]),
dict(topic='writing-in-color',title='Writing and editing in color',subtitle='Readable documents and native editor contracts share a palette.',refs=['docs/MARKDOWN-EDITORS.md','docs/TYPORA-REVIEW.md','docs/EDITOR-TARGETS.md','apt_theme/browser_themes.py'],chapters=[
('Rendering and editing have distinct contracts','A Markdown stylesheet is not a parser.','The Markdown target styles rendered document structures. The host chooses parsing and sanitization. CodeMirror targets style live editing through different native interfaces, while Typora and SiYuan use their own application contracts. Keep these distinctions visible when showing similar-looking text: a browser specimen cannot establish downstream editor acceptance or install a missing Markdown extension.','Source Markdown|Parser and sanitizer|Rendered DOM|Theme CSS'),
('Markdown scope is explicit','Wrap rendered content in .markdown-body.','Load the generated token and Markdown styles around the intended content wrapper. Optional extras and syntax styles have separate roles. Scope limits interference with unrelated page content, but host rules and inherited typography still matter. Test long tables, code, images, and nested blocks with the actual renderer used by the application.','.markdown-body|Document elements|Optional extras'),
('GFM syntax is a renderer responsibility','CSS follows emitted structures.','Tables, task lists, strikethrough, links, and fenced code rely on the host parser producing suitable HTML. The generated coverage record distinguishes supported styling from conditional and deferred behavior. Theme support for a selector does not mean raw Markdown syntax will be recognized by every renderer. Preserve this boundary in integration instructions.','Markdown syntax|Renderer support|HTML structure|Styled result'),
('Conditional HTML stays conditional','Alerts and footnotes need host support.','Alert-like blocks and footnote structures are authored separately in the preview and labeled as conditional HTML contracts. Parsing those constructs is not installed by the theme. Details, summary, keyboard tags, subscript, and superscript depend on sanitizer policy. Document the required DOM and trust boundary instead of silently describing optional HTML as universal Markdown behavior.','Optional syntax|Host extension|Sanitizer policy|Conditional DOM'),
('Sanitization belongs to the host','A CSS file does not make untrusted HTML safe.','The preview demonstrates a rendering and sanitization arrangement, but that policy is not applied automatically when the CSS is copied elsewhere. The application must choose its own handling for untrusted content, URLs, and embedded structures. Keep example code inert and escape it before highlighting. These are integration responsibilities separate from color adaptation.','Untrusted input|Host policy|Escaped code|Styled document'),
('Prism uses grammar-backed token classes','Syntax colors need the matching runtime.','The syntax target packages Prism core, pinned grammars, preserved example sources, licenses, and a hash manifest. CSS maps semantic roles to token classes. A language label alone does not execute a grammar. Load the required local dependencies in the recorded order and inspect representative highlighted tokens without running the sample application code.','Prism core|Grammar dependencies|Token classes|Theme CSS'),
('CM5 is native scoped CSS','Use the exact generation-prefixed theme identifier.','CodeMirror 5 CSS remains under .cm-s-<prefix>-<theme>. Configure that same theme name on the editor. Keep the completion popup within the themed wrapper using hintOptions.container. The preview includes an unrelated editor as an isolation control. This helps reveal selectors that accidentally affect other editors or completion surfaces.','Theme identifier|Scoped CM5 CSS|Completion container|Isolation control'),
('CM6 exports an ES-module interface','Import theme, highlightStyle, and extension.','The CodeMirror 6 module exports its dark EditorView theme, syntax HighlightStyle, and combined extension. Add the extension to EditorState rather than treating the file as CM5 CSS. Browser ES modules require local HTTP for the packaged preview. All assets remain local, but direct file opening is not a valid replacement for that module environment.','ES module|EditorView theme|HighlightStyle|EditorState'),
('Editing states deserve visual examples','Selection and search are part of readability.','The browser editor targets cover gutters, cursor, active line, selection, search matches, brackets, panels, and completion tooltips. Inspect these states while editing, not only in a static code image. A shared syntax color can behave differently on an active line or selection background, so consult the emitted validation pairs as well as the default code specimen.','Cursor|Selection|Search|Completion'),
('Typora has a native review boundary','Browser resemblance is useful but incomplete.','Typora exports native styling and paired specimens, with documented checks for opening documents, lists, code, tables, and application-specific surfaces. A browser preview can reveal layout problems, but it cannot prove that Typora loaded the theme or applied every selector. Keep manual application review separate and record the actual version and observed behavior when performed.','Native CSS|Sample document|Typora review|Recorded result'),
('Vendor rebuilds are deliberate maintenance','Normal generation needs no Node or network.','Pinned browser assets are rebuilt only through tools/browser-themes using the package lock and documented build commands. Licenses and hashes accompany the resulting files. The Python exporter verifies and copies packaged resources during normal generation. Do not run an unreviewed dependency update merely to author documentation or refresh a sample image.','Pinned lock|Maintainer rebuild|Resource hashes|Python export'),
('Read the Clojure specimens together','Compare rendering, editing, and origin records.','The copied Clojure target directories retain real generated Markdown, CM5, CM6, syntax, and Typora artifacts. Open the appropriate index, use loopback for CM6, and inspect the native integration README. Follow one code role from the semantic record into the target token JSON. Similar colors across targets do not imply identical selector or parser behavior.','Markdown|CM5|CM6|Typora'),
('Decision: keep native interfaces intact','Use each target in the environment it declares.','Preserve .markdown-body, the CM5 theme class, and CM6 named exports. These are integration contracts even when the same palette underlies them. A generic CSS merge would lose meaningful boundaries. The documentation collection supplies links and explanations without changing those interfaces.','Markdown scope|CM5 class|CM6 exports'),
('Failure: CSS cannot add syntax','Inspect coverage and host parsing first.','If footnotes or alerts appear as literal text, check the parser and sanitizer before changing colors. If CM6 fails under file opening, serve the copied files over loopback. If completion appears unthemed in CM5, inspect its container. These failure modes are environmental or structural and do not justify regenerating the palette.','Parser issue|Module environment|Popup container'),
('Evidence: preview checks have a boundary','Downstream application acceptance remains independent.','Historical browser checks exercised parsing, sanitization, highlighting, interactions, isolation, and offline requests in recorded pilots. Those records retain their dates. The collection validates copied artifacts and documentation rendering. It does not claim that a current Typora or SiYuan session activated the exported theme.','Browser pilot|Current artifacts|Native application'),
('Recovery: retain complete target trees','Pinned dependencies travel with the specimen.','Keep the original target directory and its manifest together. For a new integration revision, copy verified assets into a fresh directory and document host-specific changes separately. Do not edit vendored runtime bytes or source-run CSS to fix a documentation preview. Preserve the earlier state for a meaningful comparison.','Verified source|Complete copy|Host changes|Comparison')]),
dict(topic='visual-collection',title='Exploring the visual collection',subtitle='Palette matrices, projections, SVG scenes, and data specimens.',refs=['docs/CHROMATIC-ATLAS.md','docs/VISUAL-TARGETS.md','apt_theme/atlas.py','apt_theme/visual_targets.py'],chapters=[
('Atlas snapshots selected runs','The collection view does not regenerate themes.','The atlas command reads explicitly selected existing run data and writes a separate offline snapshot. Canonical IDs and physical run identities remain intact. Browser selections do not edit the source collection. Keep the legacy asset atlas utility separate: it catalogs artwork, while the theme-run Atlas normalizes canonical values, tokens, and provenance for exploration.','Existing runs|Snapshot|Offline explorer|Separate selection'),
('The matrix preserves exact identity','A canonical slot is local to its source palette.','Each theme contributes 32 canonical entries. Matrix order can be canonical, lightness, or hue, and the inspector retains exact IDs and RGB values. color_12 in two palettes is not necessarily the same role or color. Use the combined run and canonical ID when saving a selection so a visually similar tile cannot erase source identity.','Physical run|Canonical ID|Exact RGB|Matrix tile'),
('Filters change the view','They do not change the palette.','Search, hue families, lightness, and chroma limits help inspect the collection. Selection remains separate from canonical data. Nearest-neighbor searches use the full snapshot independently of filters, so an exact match may lie outside the current visible subset. Explain that difference when a neighbor seems absent from the displayed matrix.','Full snapshot|Visible filter|Selection tray|Neighbors'),
('Projection is a deliberate loss of dimensions','The a × b plane omits lightness.','OKLab a and b show chromatic position while omitting L. Points that overlap can have different lightness or source identity, and the view does not jitter them apart. Full nearest-neighbor distance uses all three coordinates. Use the matrix and inspector for exact selection rather than treating a two-dimensional gap as proof of perceptual distance.','L a b|a × b view|Overlapping points|Inspector'),
('Hue views need a neutral convention','Below chroma 0.02, label near-neutral.','Hue/lightness projection excludes near-neutral colors below the documented chroma threshold instead of assigning them an artificial red hue. That threshold is a display convention, not a universal color-science category. The canonical matrix still retains those colors. Explain why a projection may contain fewer points without implying that the snapshot lost palette entries.','Chroma threshold|Near-neutral label|Hue projection|Complete matrix'),
('The inspector follows recorded descendants','Token lineage differs from property consumption.','Selecting a canonical entry reveals semantic assignments and recorded target tokens. Token inspection shows source hash, requested and actual coordinates, and gamut information. This traces generation data, not every native selector or application property that consumes a token. Unknown usage remains unknown; the explorer should not fill the gap with an inferred native acceptance claim.','Canonical entry|Semantic roles|Target descendants|Usage boundary'),
('Selections are separate artifacts','Copy or export without changing canonical data.','The browser supports individual and range selections and can download a separate selection JSON. The selection schema records chosen canonical identities and source snapshot context. It is not a pipeline palette replacement and does not rewrite run files. Keep that distinction when sharing a curated set with another designer.','Matrix choice|Selection tray|Selection JSON|Unchanged source'),
('Six SVG scenes exercise different ideas','Editable geometry is useful documentation material.','The SVG target includes technical flow, editorial infographic, orbit map, icon sheet, wayfinding map, and product illustration. Scenes use local symbols, paths, clips, patterns, gradients, and presentation attributes. They are reusable examples, with fictional wayfinding and conceptual colors labeled. Browser rendering does not establish that every native importer preserves those constructs.','Flow|Infographic|Icons|Illustration'),
('Chart values are illustrative fixtures','Use exact tables when values matter.','The data target supplies 12 bars, six 24-point lines, and an 8 by 12 heatmap. Labels and repeating patterns supplement narrow categorical palettes. The heatmap values page exposes all 96 cells. These datasets demonstrate visual behavior; they are not measurements of project performance. Keep the illustrative label adjacent to chart specimens.','12 bars|6 lines|96 heatmap cells|Value table'),
('Styles and scales have separate jobs','A Matplotlib style does not install a colormap.','The mplstyle sets figure surfaces, text, axes, grids, legends, and a six-entry cycle. The scales JSON contains nine sequential stops that a consumer passes explicitly to a colormap. Authored SVG charts and optional Matplotlib renders share fixture values but are different rendering paths. A screenshot of one cannot establish acceptance of the other.','mplstyle|Color cycle|Scales JSON|Explicit colormap'),
('Neutral galleries frame themed canvases','Viewing chrome is not part of every palette.','SVG and chart gallery pages use a fixed neutral review environment, while their embedded scenes retain theme colors. This makes comparisons practical without recoloring canonical artifacts. Document which surface belongs to the gallery and which belongs to the themed canvas. Do not count the neutral gallery CSS as new canonical colors.','Neutral gallery|Themed canvas|Exact artifact|Review context'),
('Read Cloudflare as a visual case','Combine the palette plot with actual SVG and chart files.','The lightness and chromaticity figures use recorded Cloudflare canonical values. The linked SVG and data pages preserve actual generated specimens. The new figures are documentation views, not regenerated themes or a replacement Atlas implementation. Follow copied token records to inspect marks and scale origins, then use the existing Atlas command for an interactive selected-run snapshot.','Canonical data|Documentation plot|SVG specimen|Chart fixture'),
('Decision: preserve uncertainty in plots','Do not use empty space as a reason to add colors.','A sparse projection describes the selected palette in that coordinate view. It does not establish a missing hue requirement. Inspect the source image, matrix, and exact values before forming a design judgment. Preserve canonical colors and keep any new selection proposal separate from the recorded snapshot.','Projection|Source image|Exact values|Separate proposal'),
('Failure: unresolved lineage rejects the snapshot','Malformed identity cannot become a plausible graph.','Atlas validates identities, RGB agreement, and source/token origins. Missing optional records can produce findings, while malformed or unresolved provenance rejects the snapshot. Read the diagnostic rather than inventing source links. A safe explorer can display less information when evidence is absent; it must not claim unsupported completeness.','Validate identity|Resolve origins|Finding or failure'),
('Evidence: browser behavior and native import differ','An SVG can render well and still import differently.','Historical browser evidence covers interactions and rendering in recorded environments. Native SVG editors and Office may handle symbols, clipping, fonts, and gradients differently. Optional Matplotlib acceptance also depends on its actual renderer. Record these gates independently and retain illustrative data labels when exporting specimens into documentation.','Browser render|Native import|Matplotlib|Separate receipts'),
('Recovery: rebuild a snapshot from preserved runs','Keep browser selections separate from source history.','Use a fresh Atlas destination for a new selected collection. Keep the prior snapshot and its source receipts when comparison matters. Replacing an Atlas-owned directory has explicit guards, but it does not regenerate or restore theme runs. A downloaded selection JSON is a convenience artifact and cannot recover a missing canonical palette by itself.','Preserved runs|Fresh snapshot|Source receipts|Selection file')]),
dict(topic='artwork-custody',title='Artwork, conversion, and custody',subtitle='Preserve source identity while making useful new media.',refs=['docs/SVG-FINISHING.md','scripts/help.md','apt_theme/svg_converter.py','docs/ARCHITECTURE.md'],chapters=[
('Custody starts with original bytes','Keep artwork and output identities connected.','Artwork catalogs, generated runs, and converted files serve different purposes. Preserve source bytes, descriptive names, hashes, and historical evidence before making a derivative. A logo, palette board, and preview image should retain distinct roles in receipts and metadata. A visually similar file is not a verified replacement for an original.','Original artwork|Source receipt|Derivative|Role metadata'),
('ICO is a frame container','Inspect sizes and alpha in the actual output.','The converter can assemble icon frames from selected artwork. Review its documented flags and inspect the final frame set rather than assuming that a renamed PNG is an ICO. Small-size legibility and alpha behavior require visual review at native sizes. Preserve the original input and write the conversion to a fresh destination.','Source PNG|Requested frames|ICO container|Native-size review'),
('SVG wrapping preserves raster content','Embedded PNG bytes remain raster artwork.','Embed mode places the original PNG inside an SVG image element and retains its dimensions and transparency. It is useful for transport and metadata, but it does not create editable paths. Byte-preservation checks can establish that the payload survived. A browser rendering comparison addresses appearance, while native editor handling remains a separate gate.','PNG bytes|SVG image element|Preserved payload|Raster content'),
('Tracing creates paths with tradeoffs','Use the actual compatible VTracer.','Trace mode invokes the documented VTracer contract and refuses to silently fall back to wrapping. Real output can simplify textures, posterize gradients, and lose fine detail. A stub test only exercises orchestration. Review the actual original/trace comparison and record executable version, settings, and hash before describing tracing fidelity.','PNG|VTracer|Paths|Fidelity review'),
('Palette geometry uses recorded data','Do not sample a swatch to recreate its colors.','Palette finishing builds editable rectangles and labels from matching canonical or target palette data. It checks expected dimensions, fills, order, and lineage. Arbitrary PNG artwork lacks that structured contract and must use embed or trace. The finished board can differ in font metrics while preserving exact recorded color values.','Structured palette|Exact values|Editable geometry|Lineage'),
('Finishing begins with explicit selection','The default action is a read-only preview.','A finish plan lists selected assets, roles, modes, and optional descriptive identity. Paths resolve relative to the plan. Preview validates selection, structured data, dependencies, and schema without creating output assets. It cannot evaluate produced SVG content safety before SVGs exist. The eventual write is explicit and uses a fresh destination.','Selection plan|Read-only preview|Explicit write|Fresh output'),
('Run references use manifests','Logical artifact keys locate actual names.','An asset can identify a run and optional target rather than hardcoding a generated filename. The resolver checks the target inventory, identity, and hashes. Without a target, the run palette PNG is selected; with a target, logical keys identify the desired recorded artifact. Keep exact generation evidence and reject conflicting identities.','Run|Target manifest|Logical key|Verified artifact'),
('SESM remains independently governed','Finishing orchestrates the sibling tool.','The project invokes SESM-Metadata-Embedder through explicit project, standard, interpreter, configuration, and write interfaces. It supplies descriptive roles and provenance without absorbing metadata ownership. Canonical standard resources stay read-only. An installed theme wheel may need explicit sibling paths because the source checkout arrangement cannot be assumed on another machine.','Finishing|Sibling embedder|Canonical standard|Receipt'),
('Descriptive metadata is not command authority','Keep diagnostics in the receipt.','Embedded metadata identifies the asset, source, conversion method, role, and compact theme identity. Full identity-resolution evidence and tool settings stay in the finishing receipt. Internal diagnostic labels can trigger canonical safety rules when embedded, so do not weaken the validator to fit them. Metadata should describe the artwork rather than instruct the next application.','Asset description|Compact identity|Full receipt|Safety validation'),
('Schema and safety are separate results','Warnings remain visible.','A valid schema does not guarantee a clean safe-profile classification. Finishing records warnings as sesm-unverified and only clean canonical validation as sesm-safe. Safety errors abort the batch. The receipt keeps both results and exact tool/source hashes, so a downstream consumer can inspect what was established without treating a warning as success.','Schema|Safe profile|Warnings|Final classification'),
('Staging protects an entire selection','Commit only after every asset succeeds.','Finishing writes into staging, invokes metadata tooling, verifies artwork preservation, and rechecks consulted hashes before committing. Existing destinations, unsafe names, collisions, overlaps, and changed inputs fail. There is no finishing overwrite option. Keep a new destination for each revision and preserve failed diagnostics instead of modifying source runs to make a batch pass.','Stage|Convert|Validate and recheck|Commit'),
('Read Flathub as preserved artwork','Compare source, palette, and native output roles.','The copied Flathub source and generated targets remain distinct. Use the palette board to inspect exact colors, then open the linked SVG specimens to see editable examples. This volume does not claim those authored examples are traced versions of the logo. Its exercised embed example verifies payload preservation; real tracing suitability stays tied to an actual trace review.','Logo source|Palette board|Authored SVG|Conversion receipt'),
('Decision: choose the conversion mode intentionally','Fidelity, editability, and structured reconstruction differ.','Use embed when original raster fidelity matters, trace when paths are required and approximation is acceptable, and palette mode for recorded swatches. State the selected mode in filenames and receipts. Do not call a wrapped PNG vector tracing or use a generated infographic as evidence that the logo was traced accurately.','Embed|Trace|Palette geometry'),
('Failure: a missing tool is an error','Never silently change the requested mode.','A missing or incompatible tracer, invalid palette match, conflicting identity, or metadata safety error should stop the selected operation. Read the diagnostic and correct the dependency or plan in a new revision. Falling back to a different conversion mode would change the deliverable while concealing why the requested result was unavailable.','Requested mode|Dependency validation|Clear failure'),
('Evidence: preserve bytes and review appearance','These are complementary checks.','Hashes and embedded-payload comparisons establish identity and preservation. Rendered comparisons establish appearance in the tested environment. Native import and editing establish another gate. A tool version and passing schema cannot establish faithful logo tracing alone. Retain comparison images and unresolved findings with the receipt.','Hash check|Payload check|Visual review|Native import'),
('Recovery: retain the source and receipt','Derivatives can be recreated deliberately.','Keep original artwork, structured palette data, selected plan, exact tool identity, and finished output receipt together. A recovery archive should be verified before relying on it. Recreate derivatives into a fresh directory and compare them with the prior version. Historical archives remain custody evidence and are not deleted by documentation delivery.','Original|Plan and tools|Receipt|Fresh derivative')]),
dict(topic='safe-operation',title='Operating and maintaining safely',subtitle='Commands, diagnostics, staged writes, and evidence you can trust.',refs=['docs/COMMAND-CONTRACT.md','docs/TESTING-REFERENCE.md','docs/STABILIZATION-AUDIT.md','apt_theme/transactions.py'],chapters=[
('Start with the current contract','Local pilot status remains explicit.','The package is a local pilot with fifteen existing target IDs and exporter expansion paused. Read architecture, commands, output naming, and current validation before running historical maintenance tools. Older documents contain useful dated context but can describe earlier inventories. A setup recipe or passing test does not establish installation, release readiness, or native acceptance.','Current docs|Local pilot|Existing contracts|Evidence limits'),
('Use fresh destinations','Non-pipeline output directories are protected.','Generate and import require an explicit output. Existing pipeline runs need an intentional overwrite option, while non-pipeline nonempty directories are refused. Keep source input outside output. For ordinary iteration, use a dated destination so prior comparisons survive. A successful overwrite intentionally removes the previous run after the new one commits.','Input|Fresh output|Ownership guard|Completed run'),
('Machine output has a stream contract','Progress belongs on stderr.','Human completion or the JSON envelope goes to stdout. Processing progress and errors use stderr. The CTS envelope carries status, tool, version, data, warnings, and errors. A script should parse stdout and retain stderr separately instead of mixing progress into JSON. Inspect exit code as well as the presence of output files.','stdout summary|stderr progress|Exit code|Receipt'),
('Exit codes distinguish outcomes','Strict failure can leave a completed review bundle.','Exit 0 means generation completed, possibly with findings. Exit 1 indicates processing, input, or configuration failure. Strict-mode contrast failure returns 2 after completed artifacts are written; argument syntax errors also use 2. Interpret the envelope and diagnostics together rather than assuming every nonzero result means no files exist or that every zero result means visual acceptance.','0 completed|1 processing failure|2 strict or syntax'),
('Inspect before installing anything','Review artifacts are the first handoff.','Open review.html, canonical palette, semantic findings, target tokens, swatches, and validation. Verify intended identity and source hash. Contrast findings and missing conventional hues deserve explicit review. The pipeline does not install or activate themes, so native testing should consume selected files deliberately and record the application version and observed result.','Review report|Findings|Native files|Application gate'),
('Transactions separate building from replacement','A failed commit restores the prior output.','Pipeline generation assembles a sibling staging tree before committing. Shared transaction helpers support rollback when replacement fails. That protection does not retain an old run after a successful overwrite. Atlas and finishing have their own ownership rules even though they share commit machinery. Read the command-specific contract before assuming a flag is interchangeable.','Build staging|Validate|Commit|Rollback on failure'),
('Canonical and target checks differ','Preservation and readability need separate evidence.','Verify canonical IDs and RGB values for preservation. Verify target token origins, native payloads, package members, and declared contrast pairs for adaptation. Then render or import in the intended environment. A schema-valid palette can still look poor, and a good browser preview cannot establish a native editor loaded its theme. Keep those conclusions separate in receipts.','Canonical preservation|Target validation|Native package|Rendering'),
('Tests should follow the change','Run focused pipeline and converter suites.','The focused tests cover palette preservation, formats, determinism, safeguards, and conversion behavior. Broader stabilization checks compare complete generation/import fixtures and installed-wheel behavior. Run only helpers whose write scope is understood. Managed-sandbox permission failures must be distinguished from application failures and recorded honestly rather than hidden by a changing test environment.','Focused tests|Fixture comparisons|Package checks|Environment record'),
('Historical tools can mutate state','Do not use migration scripts as generic preflight.','The audit classifies migrate.py, verify.py, and governance.py as historical workflows that may overwrite outputs or records. Vendor rebuilds and metadata repair also have explicit maintenance purposes. Inspect their contracts before use. A familiar filename such as verify does not establish that a tool is read-only or safe against the reviewed collection.','Read tool contract|Identify writes|Bound destination|Record result'),
('Native acceptance is a sequence','Open, render, edit, save, and reopen are different gates.','A no-repair native open checks package acceptance. Rendering checks appearance in that application. Editing charts or adding slides through layouts, saving, and reopening test another round trip. Installation, activation, and publication are separate operational actions. Record unperformed gates rather than filling them with implied success from an earlier check.','Open|Render|Edit/save/reopen|Install or publish'),
('Recovery depends on retained evidence','Keep manifests, archives, and source receipts.','Verified recovery archives and historical outputs remain preserved. Compare current files with recorded hashes before restoration. Restore selectively when later work exists, and use a fresh destination to inspect recovered artifacts. A successful backup command is weaker evidence than verified archive contents and a tested restoration path.','Manifest|Verified archive|Restore review|Fresh destination'),
('Read Swift as an operational case','Trace its actual run into a fresh limited import.','The worked exercise imports Swift canonical colors into a fresh temporary Windows Terminal run and records status, stdout, stderr, identity, and output files. It checks canonical preservation without altering the source run or installing the theme. Other recipes remain explicitly unexecuted unless the command receipt records them. Inspect the collection commands.json for exact results.','Swift palette|Fresh import|Canonical comparison|Command receipt'),
('Decision: evidence drives the claim','Report only the gate actually observed.','Use clear labels for implemented behavior, historical recorded evidence, current verification, native acceptance pending, and proposed work. This prevents a polished PDF from making the project sound more released than it is. The collection documents the current pilot while keeping application-specific acceptance limits visible.','Implementation|Recorded evidence|Current check|Pending gate'),
('Failure: permission errors need diagnosis','Do not change source behavior to hide an environment restriction.','A sandbox can block temp paths, application COM, or browser caches independently of the code under review. Use a writable isolated directory and repeat only the affected check in an appropriate authorized environment. Keep the original failure and resulting verification method in the receipt. Do not present an unexecuted native gate as passed.','Environment error|Bounded retry|Recorded method'),
('Evidence: preserve the test log','Counts without context are easy to misread.','Retain command, interpreter, date, exit code, and full output for each focused test run. Link actual logs from the collection index. Documentation rendering checks do not renew all historical engine acceptance. When a failure remains, describe its effect on the delivery and keep the evidence instead of replacing the receipt with a summary that hides it.','Command|Environment|Exit code|Log'),
('Recovery: revise without erasing history','Make a new collection for the next edition.','This collection is a dated documentation snapshot. Future authoring should capture fresh source and template inventories into a new sibling directory and compare intentional changes. Preserve the current ZIP and validation records. Project identity, ownership, and roots remain unchanged, so parent registration updates are unnecessary unless a later task actually changes them.','Dated collection|New sibling|Fresh inventory|Retained history')])
]
